#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# THE SESSION'S TICK. Detach this and the page has a frame edge.
#
#     detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=<Surface>
#
# It was a stream edge -- `view.ProduceFrames() -> view.ConsumeFrames()` -- and
# the split was real: one half owned TIMING, the other owned RECORDING, which
# kept every queue-touching call on one thread while getting frames off the
# poll thread.
#
# WHAT IT COST was a thread each, and the producer's came out of the ThreadPool:
# a stream body is enqueued there, and a standing one never gives the worker
# back. That is how a pool acquires a MINIMUM size, and how an image with two
# standing producers and one worker deadlocks -- the second body queued behind
# one that never returns. A floor set by whatever a script opens is not a
# tuning parameter, it is a trap.
#
# So the clock became a number on the Presentable family and the recording
# became a step on it (ontology/PresentableBase.h). There is no boundary left
# between them, so there is no ring, no producer and no pool occupant -- one
# loop, on the thread this `detach` asked for.
#
# IT DRIVES MORE THAN THIS SURFACE. RunFrames advances every entity that claims
# Animated (ontology/Animated.h), so the toolbar's click-and-hold repeat and the
# layer window's isolation fade ride this same tick. A session needs exactly one
# caller of it; a session with no surface at all can drive the family from its
# own loop instead.
#
# The argument in the other direction still holds: Vulkan is happy to submit and
# present from a thread that did not create the window, GLFW is not happy to be
# polled from one that did not. Nothing here touches PollEvents.

requires view [Surface, Presentable]

# The argument is the pacing in ms. Zero means unpaced -- present on every visit
# and let this loop's rate set the frame rate, which is the honest way to ask how
# fast the pipeline can actually go.
view.RunFrames(16)
